{{- /*
  ~/.pi/agent/workflows/settings.json — pi-workflows machine-wide settings.

  This file exists for exactly one key. pi-workflows >= 1.6.0 stopped letting a
  workflow script name an Agent tier: a script names a *strength* — `low`,
  `medium`, `high`, that package's own word for how much effort a step deserves
  — and `strengths` is the only thing that binds one to a key in the Agent tier
  catalogue in subagents.json.tmpl. Read the long note there before editing
  either file; the two are one policy split across two packages.

  Only the global file is written here. `/workflows strength` and every other
  in-app settings edit go to `<agent dir>/workflows/projects/<project>/settings.json`
  (saveWorkflowSettings always writes project scope), so a full-file template is
  safe at this path — unlike dot_claude/settings.json, which the app rewrites in
  place and which therefore has to be a modify_ script.

  WHY THIS IS PINNED RATHER THAN LEFT ABSENT
  An absent file does not mean "no mappings". pi-workflows computes a default
  table at run start — each strength on the catalogue tier of the same name,
  wherever this host defines one — which on this machine is exactly the identity
  table written below. So this file changes no cost today, and that is the point:
  it states the mapping instead of deriving it. The derivation is silent when it
  stops matching. Rename or drop a profile in subagents.json and the default
  table simply stops mapping that strength, every call naming it falls through
  to pi-subagents' managed `medium` fallback, and nothing in this repository
  changed to say so. A written entry whose tier the catalogue no longer defines
  is reported once at run start instead, which is the whole difference.

  WHY IT IS NOT A NO-OP
  `low -> low` in a written table is a real entry, not a redundant one. A table
  that is present replaces the shipped default outright rather than merging into
  it, so a strength omitted here is *unmapped* even though this host defines a
  tier of the same name. Deleting a line below is therefore a behaviour change,
  not a cleanup.

  WHAT THIS FILE MAY NEVER CONTAIN
  A model or a thinking level. Every value below is a key in the one catalogue in
  subagents.json.tmpl; pi-subagents still owns every model, every thinking level,
  and the only resolveAgentTier(). That is the line between this key and the
  retired `workflow.tiers`, which carried its own model/thinking values and so
  became a second model policy behind a second resolver.

  HOW TO RE-PRICE WORKFLOW WORK
  Not by editing a rung below — `low` is shared with the Explore agent, whose
  frontmatter in dot_pi/agent/agents/Explore.md sets `tier: low` so read-only
  search stays cheap, and with every ordinary spawn that names it. (That key was
  missing until 2026-09-06, so Explore had been silently taking `defaultTier`;
  the other five agents in that directory still do, deliberately.) Re-pricing a rung drags all of them. Add a new profile to
  agentTiers.profiles in subagents.json.tmpl and point a strength at it here.
  Any key the catalogue defines is legal; it is checked against the live
  catalogue at run start, one hop, never chained.

  Costs to read off subagents.json.tmpl for the built-in workflows as of 1.6.0:
  the `verify` and `judgePanel` quality helpers request `low`, and
  `completenessCheck` requests `medium`. A tier a call requests outranks
  defaultTier, so those do not follow it. A call naming no strength sends no
  tier at all and takes the agent's ordinary default.

  Both of those rungs were repriced on 2026-09-06 and this table is what carries
  the new price into every workflow run, so re-read them rather than trusting a
  remembered number. On the private machine `low` went luna/xhigh -> luna/max
  ($0.33 -> $0.53 per task) and `medium` went luna/max -> gpt-6-astra/high
  ($0.53 -> $2.84). A `completenessCheck`-heavy workflow therefore costs several
  times what it did, while `verify` and `judgePanel` moved little. On work the
  same two rungs are luna/xhigh and luna/max, i.e. one notch cheaper each.

  Deliberately not set here: progressMode, maxAgentsShown, effort, and the
  keyword-trigger keys. Those are presentation and session preferences the app
  edits per project; leaving them absent keeps this file about routing only.
*/ -}}
{
  "strengths": {
    "low": "low",
    "medium": "medium",
    "high": "high"
  }
}
